      "id": "ojdt0zg",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "8. Extraction of Metals"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Frequently tested exceptions: Mercury (liquid metal), Iodine (lustrous non-metal), and Graphite (conducting non-metal).",
        "Displacement reactions and predicting product outcomes based on the Reactivity Series are high-yield exam topics.",
        "Amphoteric oxides (such as Aluminum Oxide and Zinc Oxide) that react with both acids and bases appear repeatedly in competitive exams."
      ],
      "id": "ghvtrnp"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2rz3qtf",
      "content": "• Highly reactive metals are extracted by electrolysis."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "dh21liv",
      "content": "• Moderately reactive metals are extracted by reduction using carbon."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ry1vvte",
      "content": "• Less reactive metals are often found in free state."
    },
    {
      "id": "d019akz",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Important Facts"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "z7zfxa7",
      "content": "• Metals are good conductors of heat and electricity."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3oe09hz",
      "content": "• Non-metals are generally poor conductors."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "vy161cz",
      "content": "• Mercury is the only metal that is liquid at room temperature."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "zvmj7lm",
      "content": "• Gold and silver are least reactive metals."
    }
  ],
